WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.590
A major thing I’ve discussed recently is
deck consistency, and I’ve been surprised

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:07.629
by how much feedback I’ve gotten, in multiple
dimensions—I’ve had people telling me

00:00:07.629 --> 00:00:11.370
that I’m besmirching the spirit of a singleton
format, but I’ve also had a lot of people

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.960
who were interested and genuinely curious
about how to make a deck consistent.

00:00:14.960 --> 00:00:19.170
So, I figure for this video I can spend 10
minutes yelling at that first group about

00:00:19.170 --> 00:00:20.500
how wrong they are.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:25.180
Alright, listen here ya bozos—no, those
people are entitled to their deckbuilding

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:27.380
preferences, and I wish them well.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:32.200
I actually want to give a more detailed look
at deck consistency as a subject, with an

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:37.570
emphasis on the how—what’s the best way
to turn a floppy pile of jank into a well-oiled

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:38.760
machine?

00:00:38.760 --> 00:00:42.160
To get there, I want to start by clarifying
my terms.

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.200
I throw around the phrase “consistent deck”
a lot, and one interpretation of a consistent

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.260
deck is one that does the same thing every
game.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:54.820
I think this is a very incomplete definition
for what I’m talking about, and the definition

00:00:54.820 --> 00:01:00.410
I’m going to use instead is that a consistent
deck is one that consistently does something,

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:04.580
and whose things it does don’t vary all
that much in power level.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.440
This often means decks with multiple avenues
of play whose construction is good enough

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.100
that the different options all feel valuable.

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:16.430
The three decks I mentioned in my last video,
my $25 Rakdos deck, my Meria deck, and my

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:20.100
Glissa deck, all basically do variations on
a theme.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:23.730
With Glissa, the deck might take different
opening plays depending on the game, but at

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:29.310
the end of the day its general game plan will
be ramp, play big stuff, and gain board dominance.

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:33.500
Similarly, with my Meria deck, the deck is
always looking to play a bunch of equipments

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:37.149
and swing big in the midgame, but that can
take a lot of different shapes.

00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:40.939
If you want to learn more about the Meria
deck, I recently posted a ten minute deck

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:45.810
tech over on my patreon that covers the process
of how I built it, a breakdown of the list,

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.829
and some takeaways I had from the deckbuilding
process.

00:01:48.829 --> 00:01:53.050
I’ve mentioned a handful of reasons I like
consistent decks in the past, and I’ll give

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.460
a brief rehash here.

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:59.719
From a table politics standpoint, people will
remember the most powerful moments of the

00:01:59.719 --> 00:02:03.659
deck—your spooky pop-off win you can only
pull off 10% of the time will be remembered

00:02:03.659 --> 00:02:09.170
alongside a more consistent deck’s quite
solid gameplan it pulls off 75% of the time,

00:02:09.170 --> 00:02:12.890
and this will make your deck seem disproportionately
scary.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.950
Even if table memory isn’t a concern, from
a fun standpoint I like to have my deck be

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.420
doing something every game.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.050
This is doubly true when I’m playing with
new people, where my deck failing to get off

00:02:23.050 --> 00:02:27.849
the ground will look an awful lot like I just
built a bad deck, rather than being seen as

00:02:27.849 --> 00:02:31.170
the unfortunate sequence of draws it truly
was.

00:02:31.170 --> 00:02:36.120
To be clear, this is absolutely an ego thing,
but fortunately for me, for the moment we

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.340
all have human brains that do silly human
brain things, so I know a decent portion of

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:42.909
you share in my vanity at the table.

00:02:42.909 --> 00:02:46.960
Now that I’ve got the what and why out of
the way, let’s talk how.

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:51.930
For starters, I think one of the easier forms
of consistency to nail is what I like to call

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:54.269
“rollout consistency”.

00:02:54.269 --> 00:02:58.780
This type of consistency basically asks the
question, “do I do what I want to do on

00:02:58.780 --> 00:03:01.120
the first several turns of the game?”

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:05.260
That “what” can vary deck-to-deck, and
obviously you won’t play a turn 1 Sol Ring

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:09.160
every game, but you can check to make sure
that your deck is generally doing at least

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:11.140
*something* that feels good.

00:03:11.140 --> 00:03:15.750
The best way of doing this is solo playtesting
a deck a whole bunch, and I generally playtest

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.570
my decks quite a lot before spending any money
on them.

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:23.840
However, this can be time consuming, and one
way of reducing this time is having a baseline

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.230
of probabilistic sense for the cards in your
deck so you’re at least in the general ballpark

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:29.750
with things.

00:03:29.750 --> 00:03:34.260
Anybody who plays a lot of card games will
have this intuitively hammered in to an extent,

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:38.250
but just for fun here I’m gonna show you
exactly what I mean.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:43.170
Let’s say I want to look at how often I
get a particular type of card by a particular

00:03:43.170 --> 00:03:46.900
turn, using the example of a Goreclaw deck
I’ve been building.

00:03:46.900 --> 00:03:50.680
The deck always wants to play the commander
by turn 3, and this means that it needs to

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.069
get a piece of ramp by turn 2, every game.

00:03:54.069 --> 00:03:58.790
Disregarding mulligans, lands, and other factors,
the probability of this happening is modeled

00:03:58.790 --> 00:04:03.930
by this function, where X is the number of
ramp spells in my deck, and it can be used

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:08.459
to graph out how likely it is to achieve this
goal based on that number.

00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:13.120
As we can see on the graph, if I have 10 ramp
spells in the deck there’s a 63% chance

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:19.229
of having one on turn 2, if there’s 15 in
deck I have a 79% chance, if there’s 20

00:04:19.229 --> 00:04:21.840
I have an 88% chance, and so on.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:26.560
Alright, now let’s instead say I really
want to see ramp in the opening hand, and

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:28.710
will happily mulligan to get there.

00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:34.120
If I decide to run 15 ramp spells, then for
a given seven card hand, there’s a 69.6%

00:04:34.120 --> 00:04:38.470
chance of having at least one ramp spell,
and this graph lays out that probability based

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.320
on the number of hands drawn.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:46.610
For one hand drawn it’s 69.6%, for two hands
drawn it’s 90.8%, for three hands drawn

00:04:46.610 --> 00:04:49.840
it’s 97.2% of the time, and so forth.

00:04:49.840 --> 00:04:53.669
A simple benchmark you can reference is that
you need 10 of a card in a deck to have it

00:04:53.669 --> 00:04:57.220
show up in a given hand more than half the
time.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:01.440
You could also do this sort of modeling for
a combo deck—for this hypothetical persist

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.090
combo deck running no tutors and no partial
pieces, in order to have a better-than-half

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:10.780
chance of drawing one of each piece to have
the combo, you need to draw roughly half the

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:15.910
deck, and there’s about a one in 400 chance
of drawing it in the opening hand.

00:05:15.910 --> 00:05:20.470
Another possible calculation would be modeling
land drops given a certain land count.

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:24.030
By showing you this kind of modeling, I’m
certainly not trying to say that you should

00:05:24.030 --> 00:05:28.540
go out and get a degree in math before building
a deck—I almost never run this sort of calculation

00:05:28.540 --> 00:05:30.200
while deckbuilding.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:34.720
What I am trying to say, though, is that it’s
good to have a rough idea of the actual numbers

00:05:34.720 --> 00:05:36.350
of the situation.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:41.590
I think a lot of people tend to underestimate
how many cards 100 actually is, and that tends

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:46.569
to result in people putting in less cards
with a particular effect than would be ideal.

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:50.500
This also then has other effects on the card
quality—you might build out the core of

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:54.040
the deck and then go “wow, I’ve got so
many slots left!

00:05:54.040 --> 00:05:56.860
I could put all sorts of stuff in here.”

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:01.919
I’ve been there, and let me tell you, those
decks end up turning out way worse than decks

00:06:01.919 --> 00:06:05.509
where I’m sweating and dying trying to find
space for everything.

00:06:05.509 --> 00:06:10.220
When slots are plentiful, filler that weakens
the deck as a whole will work its way in,

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:16.280
cards where you go “oh this seems decent
in a couple niche situations” or “oh yeah

00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:20.230
this card has slight synergies with some other
elements of the deck.”

00:06:20.230 --> 00:06:23.970
These cards are probably there in place of
other, better-suited cards.

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:28.550
A good first step toward reducing this sort
of filler is to categorize your deck, and

00:06:28.550 --> 00:06:30.800
think about the categories.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:34.849
Different decks will use different systems,
but four supporting categories I usually have

00:06:34.849 --> 00:06:41.069
are Ramp, Draw, Removal, and Protection, with
those last two categories sometimes combined

00:06:41.069 --> 00:06:44.740
for decks whose interaction mainly consists
of counterspells.

00:06:44.740 --> 00:06:48.539
Separating these out and making sure you have
enough of each is a good first step of making

00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:52.230
sure that you’re spending slots on things
that will actually make your deck better,

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.230
rather than throwing in some random pet enchantments.

00:06:55.230 --> 00:06:59.310
Within the categories, there might be the
question of whether a card truly belongs there.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:02.030
For example, is Gilded Lotus ramp?

00:07:02.030 --> 00:07:06.520
If you’re accelerating to ten or more mana
to play a lot of big threats, yes it is.

00:07:06.520 --> 00:07:10.700
If you’re running ramp so that you can hit
a 6 mana commander consistently, no it’s

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.150
not ramp.

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:15.880
For draw, you should consider the difference
between conditional draw, general purpose

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:20.199
draw, and filtering, since if your deck relies
too heavily on conditional draw, you’ll

00:07:20.199 --> 00:07:23.420
have games where you get behind and then stay
behind.

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:27.720
Once you’ve figured out your categories
and considered what sorts of cards *should*

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:32.210
make up a given category, the next question
might be how much of each to run, and this

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:34.039
will vary from deck to deck.

00:07:34.039 --> 00:07:38.729
Things like draw and ramp can be tested via
playtesting, but reactive elements will be

00:07:38.729 --> 00:07:40.650
trickier to figure out.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:44.669
For removal I’d recommend starting between
8 and 14, and adjust from there keeping in

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:49.940
mind that slower decks and lower-velocity
decks will usually want more removal than

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:53.729
faster decks and higher-velocity decks will.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:57.780
Protection is the most variable of all—I
run 0 in a couple scrappy decks that don’t

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:01.759
give a shit about anything, and 12 in the
Meria deck whose supporting categories are

00:08:01.759 --> 00:08:06.069
currently on screen, where my commander staying
alive an extra turn might mean a dead opponent

00:08:06.069 --> 00:08:07.600
and a bunch of value.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.610
How much protection is correct for your deck
will depend on how much it cares about its

00:08:10.610 --> 00:08:14.991
commander and other key cards surviving—
and if you care a lot about that, I’d like

00:08:14.991 --> 00:08:19.110
to remind you that if you’re only running
the two boots, your odds of drawing protection

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:24.449
in your first five draws of the game of the
game are [tapping] a hair under 23%.

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:26.889
That’s not great!

00:08:26.889 --> 00:08:31.990
A helpful side effect of categorizing is that
it will naturally leave some cards in undeniably

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:33.460
naked view.

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:36.820
If a card doesn’t fit into one of your key
categories, you should be asking yourself

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:41.170
what its purpose is, and the answer should
be simple and straightforward.

00:08:41.170 --> 00:08:45.290
If the answer involves scratching your head
and vaguely gesturing at a couple other cards,

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:48.850
that’s a card that deserves a more robust
evaluation.

00:08:48.850 --> 00:08:52.910
In my mind, the optimal state of a decklist
is one whose components exist in comfortable

00:08:52.910 --> 00:08:58.400
tension in your mind—where there are constantly
questions in the back of your mind like “hmmm,

00:08:58.400 --> 00:09:02.850
should I swap out a ramp spell and a finisher
slot for two more draw spells?”

00:09:02.850 --> 00:09:07.080
The answer to that can be no, but asking that
kind of question in the first place will improve

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:10.320
your decks and make you better at deckbuilding.

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:13.560
A final type of consistency I want to talk
about is one that’s complex enough that

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:18.589
I might make a full video about it at some
point, and that is situational consistency.

00:09:18.589 --> 00:09:22.671
By this phrase I made up on the spot I mean
a deck that plays relatively smoothly in a

00:09:22.671 --> 00:09:26.820
bunch of different situations—you could
also call this being reactive and/or being

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:29.290
resilient depending on context.

00:09:29.290 --> 00:09:34.140
This will generally mean devoting deck space
to specific types of suppressive or protective

00:09:34.140 --> 00:09:38.459
cards, and the best way to allow a deck to
have easy access to situational cards without

00:09:38.459 --> 00:09:43.040
destroying the deck’s general consistency
is tutors.

00:09:43.040 --> 00:09:46.790
This could mean running a bunch of the black
classics, but those tend to be expensive,

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:51.301
either money-wise or mana-wise, so for a deck
looking to run a bunch of tutors, a natural

00:09:51.301 --> 00:09:54.970
place to end up is often what’s called a
“toolbox” deck.

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:58.880
This refers to a deck with a number of conditional
tutors that can each grab cards useful in

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:00.860
several different situations.

00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:06.350
A couple quintessential toolbox cards are
Birthing Pod and Yisan, the Wanderer Bard,

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:10.320
which work well in decks with a lot of card
diversity at each mana cost.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:14.000
There are some really powerful toolbox decks
out there, as higher powers are where being

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.050
able to consistently find tech pieces and
answers really shines.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:21.940
For a couple examples of toolbox decks at
a lower power, I can talk about my Sharuum

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:25.440
Slide deck, and my friend Mr Hans’ Vadrok
deck.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.140
In Sharuum Slide, the primary objective of
the toolbox is grabbing Astral Slide, but

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:34.590
there are a ton of other options embedded
into this enchantment, artifact, and wizard-based

00:10:34.590 --> 00:10:40.600
tutoring framework—board wipes, recursion
spells, draw spells, life gain, and so forth.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:44.380
This means that the deck can flow nicely once
it gets into the midgame, even though the

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.300
“interesting” mana base can often lead
to slow early games.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:51.050
In the Vadrok deck, equipments are the primary
card type that the tutor base is oriented

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:56.220
around, with the equipment base containing
value, protection, defense, graveyard hate,

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:57.559
and Sunforger.

00:10:57.559 --> 00:11:01.220
With Sunforger, the deck can hit a ton of
different cards, including a convoluted infinite

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:07.090
combo, which results in a mutate deck that’s
a little strange, but very fun and flexible.

00:11:07.090 --> 00:11:11.529
As an end note to this section, I should probably
mention: I know that a lot of people dislike

00:11:11.529 --> 00:11:16.690
tutors, and I think a lot of that dislike
comes from a particular type of usage.

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:21.980
Somebody tutors up a power card or a couple
combo pieces, and then explodes the table.

00:11:21.980 --> 00:11:27.320
This might feel bland to play against, but
I want to pose a question—is it more interesting

00:11:27.320 --> 00:11:30.589
when a deck draws into that explosive win
out of pure luck?

00:11:30.589 --> 00:11:35.000
It’ll happen less often without tutors,
sure, but having the question of whether you’ll

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:38.910
lose to a two card infinite combo be up to
a roll of the dice rather than it being tutored

00:11:38.910 --> 00:11:42.460
for doesn’t really seem better in any meaningful
sense.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:45.920
If anything, it makes it more difficult to
run counterplay for that sort of deck—a

00:11:45.920 --> 00:11:50.440
stax piece you throw in will sit un-utilized
the 85% of the time that they’re doing something

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:51.790
else.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:56.710
My advice is this—if you don’t like a
particular type of wincon or a particular

00:11:56.710 --> 00:12:00.281
power level, that’s fine, and there’s
probably a different table out there that

00:12:00.281 --> 00:12:01.820
you’ll like better.

00:12:01.820 --> 00:12:04.959
Alright, that’s this video coming to an
end.

00:12:04.959 --> 00:12:09.250
I have two things to mention here—one, I
made a website, and put a couple probability

00:12:09.250 --> 00:12:13.850
calculators up on it because I was bored and
wanted an excuse to practice my JavaScript.

00:12:13.850 --> 00:12:16.720
So you can visit there if you want to know
the probability of drawing a card type by

00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:21.640
a given turn, or the average turn you’ll
miss a land drop with a given land count.

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:22.970
Oooh, turn seven?

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:24.510
That’s pretty cool.

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:29.220
Secondly, I feel I should reiterate—as with
my last video, this is a style of deck building

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:33.380
that you can engage in, but not every deck
will be well-served by being transparent or

00:12:33.380 --> 00:12:35.389
maximizing consistency.

00:12:35.389 --> 00:12:36.480
Just leaving that out there.

00:12:36.480 --> 00:13:04.930
Anyway, here’s a preview of the Meria deck
tech I recently posted 

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:36.500
to my patreon:

